From ff97e057c90c80149abfd43bac3a666d54444048 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: crazynucfan <127292955+crazynucfan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:13:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: rhodep_defconfig: pseudo-NMI

About 1 in 5 kexec'd kernels on rhodep hung in their first 50 ms: CPU 6,
the first Cortex-A78, came up ("Booted secondary processor") and then
took no interrupt at all, neither its timer nor IPIs, so the first
cross-CPU call (a static key enable at crc_t10dif_mod_init) waited
forever. Every one of the 8 captured failures was CPU 6. The previous
kernel's handover was clean (every core off in 0 ms), and cold boots are
not affected.

With pseudo-NMI (and irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1 on the command line)
the hang did not occur in 36 launches (the rate before would have given
about 7). A plausible reading: pseudo-NMI masks interrupts through the
GIC priority mask (ICC_PMR_EL1) and rewrites it on every enable, while
without it the mask is written once at bring-up, so a CPU interface left
masking everything (by firmware restoring stale state after the kexec)
sleeps in WFI for good. It also gives the lockup detector NMI
backtraces, which this investigation lacked.

The option only takes effect with irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1, which the
kexec loader passes on rhodep (PHONE_KEXEC_ARGS in /etc/default/phone-boot).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/configs/rhodep_defconfig | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/configs/rhodep_defconfig b/arch/arm64/configs/rhodep_defconfig
index 5af3e701e..8de656ea9 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/configs/rhodep_defconfig
+++ b/arch/arm64/configs/rhodep_defconfig
@@ -1203,6 +1203,7 @@ CONFIG_CORESIGHT_STM=m
 CONFIG_CORESIGHT_CPU_DEBUG=m
 CONFIG_CORESIGHT_CTI=m
 CONFIG_MEMTEST=y
+CONFIG_ARM64_PSEUDO_NMI=y
 CONFIG_PANIC_ON_OOPS=y
 CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT=10
 CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
-- 
2.47.3

